COMMAND NAME: mppdeletepart.sh

Drops date partitioned child tables from a parent table that was 
previously partitioned using mppdeletepart.sh or mppaddpart.sh 
(used to maintain a rolling window of date partitioned data). 
This script does not support numeric range or list partitioned tables.


*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

mppdeletepart.sh -t <parent_table> -r <rolling_window_count> 
[-s <fallback_table_name>] [-f] [-q] [-a] [-l <log_dir>] [-D] 
[-d <master_data_directory>] [-x <database>]

mppdeletepart.sh -? | -v


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The mppdeletepart.sh script drops child tables from a parent table 
that was partitioned using mppdeletepart.sh or mppaddpart.sh. This is 
useful if you have a date partitioned table and want to maintain a 
rolling window of data, for example the last 18 months. The script will 
drop the oldest child table partitions (and associated indexes and rewrite 
rules) that are outside of the specified rolling window. The rolling 
window start date is calculated from the current database date. This script 
can only be used on date partitioned tables (parent tables partitioned 
on a timestamp or date column). It does not support numeric range or 
list partitioned tables. 


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-t <parent_table>

Required. The name of the parent table from which you want to drop 
child tables.


-r <rolling_window_count>

Required. The number of most recent months, days, hours you want to keep. 
Older child table partitions outside this window will be dropped. The 
rolling window start date is calculated from the current database date.


-s <fallback_table_name>

Specifies the name of a table where child table records will be copied 
before the child table is dropped. This provides some fall back in case 
you unintentionally delete partitioned data you wanted to keep.


-f (force cascaded delete)

If you have a nested partition design, this will force the deletion of 
child tables that are also parents to other tables (DROP CASCADE). 
The default is to not allow the removal of child tables that are 
also parents to other tables.


-q (no output)

Do not output messages to the screen. Write messages to log file only.


-a (do not prompt)

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.


-l <log_directory>

Specifies an alternative directory to write the log file. By default 
log files are written to ~/gpAdminLogs.


-D (debug)

Sets logging level to debug.


-? (help)

Displays the online help.


-v (show script version)

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and check sum of this script.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Drop all monthly partitioned tables that are outside the 18 month 
rolling window (and force the deletion of any nested child tables 
on the months that are dropped).

mppdeletepart.sh -t sales -r 18 -f


